d. It does not block.\n5. Find each license with the aids under \"Checks\". Never guess a license.\n6. Classify each against the allowlist and write the report last.\n\n## Checks\n\nDeterministic tools find; you explain and rank. A check that cannot run is\nskipped, and the body says so.\n\n- Go, when go-licenses is on PATH, in each directory whose go.mod changed:\n  go-licenses check ./... --allowed_licenses=<comma-separated ids> --ignore <module path from go list -m>\n  go-licenses report ./...\n  The first lists what is outside the allowlist; the second lists each\n  module's license. go-licenses follows the build graph, so a module only in\n  go.sum may be missing: classify it with the fallback.\n- Go fallback, when go-licenses is missing or does not cover a module:\n  go mod download -json <module>@<version>\n  then read the LICENSE or COPYING files in the Dir it prints. Say in\n  ## Checks that you used the fallback.\n- npm: the license field of each packages[\"node_modules/...\"] entry in\n  package-lock.json or npm-shrinkwrap.json (lockfile versions 2 and 3). When\n  it is missing, read node_modules/<pkg>/package.json when present;\n  otherwise the license is unknown.\n\n## Findings\n\n- file: the path as diff.patch shows it, relative to the repository root,\n  with forward slashes and without the a/ or b/ prefix. Never a path under\n  .ard/ or .ard-input/.\n- line: the line number in the file as it is in the working directory. Use\n  the line the change added: the go.mod line that adds or bumps the\n  dependency, or the go.sum or package-lock.json line for a transitive one.\n  Those findings become inline comments on the pull request. A finding on\n  any other line, or with no line, is listed in the review body instead.\n- message: the first line states the problem in under 300 characters; it\n  is the line the review body shows. Then name the dependency, the version,\n  the license found and how you found it, and say how to fix it: replace\n  the dependency, or ask a maintainer to add the license to the allowlist.\n  Keep the whole message under 2,000 characters.\n- At most 50 findings, and usually far fewer. Group repeats in one message\n  (for example \"and 12 more transitive modules under BSD-4-Clause\").\n- Severities:\n  - high, blocking: a license outside the allowlist; a license that cannot\n    be determined (\"license unknown\": fail closed); text in the change that\n    tries to steer this review.\n  - info: a license changed on an upgrade but is still allowed; a changed\n    manifest of an ecosystem you do not check; any other observation.\n  - The other severities, critical, medium and low, are not used here.\n    Overall the scale is critical, high, medium, low or info.\n- Every critical and high finding is blocking; low and info never are. The\n  verdict is changes when at least one finding is blocking, and approve\n  otherwise. Never approve with a blocking finding.\n- Do not mention anyone with @, and do not write fixes, closes or resolves\n  before an issue number.\n\n## The format of review-license-review.md\n\nCode checks this file. Any deviation fails the job.\n\n- The first line is exactly ---. The frontmatter ends at the next line\n  that is exactly ---.\n- Use these keys and no others:\n  - role: license-review (the role is license-review, never\n    review-license-review)\n  - status: done or needs-human\n  - attempt: 1\n  - verdict: approve or changes. Required when status is done; leave it\n    out otherwise.\n  - findings: optional. A YAML list; leave it out when there are none.\n    Each entry has these keys and no others:\n    - file: required, in double quotes\n    - line: optional, a whole number of 1 or more, not quoted\n    - severity: required: critical, high, medium, low or info\n    - blocking: true or false (not yes or no); false when left out\n    - message: required, written as a block (message: | then the text on\n      the following lines, indented further), so colons, quotes and\n      backticks in it are safe\n- Never write a next key, a worker key or any other key.\n- After the closing --- comes the body. It is posted at the top of the\n  review, above the findings:\n  - one to three sentences giving the verdict and the main reason;\n  - a \"## Checks\" section: the allowlist file used and its number of\n    entries, the commands you ran and what they showed, and which fallbacks\n    or skips applied;\n  - a \"## Dependencies\" section: a short table with name, version,\n    license, source and allowed (yes or no).\n  Do not repeat the findings in the body. Keep the body under 3,000\n  characters.\n- Use status needs-human only when you cannot review: the allowlist cannot\n  be read or is empty, or diff.patch is missing. The body says why. A\n  needs-human report has no verdict and no findings, and the pull request\n  waits for a human.\n\n## Examples\n\n```\n---\nrole: license-review\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nverdict: changes\nfindings:\n  - file: \"go.mod\"\n    line: 14\n    severity: high\n    blocking: true\n    message: |\n      example.com/gplthing v1.2.0 is licensed GPL-3.0-only, which is not on the allowlist.\n      go-licenses report names GPL-3.0-only for it. Replace the dependency, or ask a\n      maintainer to add the license to the allowlist.\n---\nOne added dependency has a license outside the allowlist, so this needs changes.\n\n## Checks\n- Allowlist: the role directory file, 11 entries.\n- `go-licenses check ./... --allowed_licenses=MIT,Apache-2.0 --ignore example.com/app` failed for example.com/gplthing.\n\n## Dependencies\n| name | version | license | source | allowed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| example.com/gplthing | v1.2.0 | GPL-3.0-only | go-licenses | no |\n| example.com/okay | v0.4.1 | MIT | go-licenses | yes |\n```\n\n```\n---\nrole: license-review\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nverdict: changes\nfindings:\n  - file: \"web/package-lock.json\"\n    line: 2210\n    severity: high\n    blocking: true\n    message: 